          ],
          "grounding_supports": [
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                0,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": null,
                "end_index": 187,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "When someone is feeling sick, particularly with nausea, food poisoning, or a sudden fever, their face often undergoes a dramatic transformation, turning pale or even looking a bit \"green\""
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                2,
                3
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 308,
                "end_index": 476,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "### Part 1: Why We Turn Pale (The Physiology)\nThe primary reason you look pale (a medical state known as **pallor**) when sick is a shift in where your blood is flowing"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                3,
                4
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 479,
                "end_index": 734,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Vasoconstriction (Blood Redirection):** When your body is fighting off an infection, feeling extremely nauseous, or experiencing physical stress, your autonomic nervous system (specifically the sympathetic \"fight-or-flight\" system) goes into high gear"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                5,
                6
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 736,
                "end_index": 813,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "It narrows the blood vessels near the surface of your skin (vasoconstriction)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                3,
                4
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 816,
                "end_index": 1067,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Prioritizing Vital Organs:** By constricting these superficial vessels, your body diverts warm, oxygen-rich red blood away from non-essential areas like your face and directs it inward toward vital organs (like your heart, brain, and deep muscles)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1069,
                "end_index": 1204,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Because the reddish-pink hue of blood is what normally gives your skin its healthy \"flush,\" removing it leaves you looking starkly pale"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                8
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1206,
                "end_index": 1305,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Dehydration and Low Blood Pressure:** Illnesses (especially stomach bugs) can cause dehydration"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                8,
                9
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1307,
                "end_index": 1448,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This lowers your overall blood volume and drops your blood pressure, meaning there is even less blood circulating to the surface of your skin"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                1,
                10
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1456,
                "end_index": 1634,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "### Part 2: Why We Turn \"Green\" (The Optics)\nWhile your face does not actually secrete green pigment when you feel nauseous, you can genuinely appear to have a greenish-gray cast"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                11,
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1636,
                "end_index": 1706,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "This is a visual illusion created by color mixing and human perception"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                12,
                13
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1709,
                "end_index": 1856,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **The Physics of Color Subtraction:** Human skin has natural yellow and warm undertones (caused by melanin and dietary pigments like carotenoids)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 1858,
                "end_index": 2013,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Normally, the bright red of oxygenated blood flowing right beneath the skin masks these undertones, blending them into a healthy peach, pink, or warm brown"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2015,
                "end_index": 2122,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "When vasoconstriction takes the red blood out of the equation, you are left with only the yellow skin tones"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                14,
                7
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2323,
                "end_index": 2465,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "When the red blood in your capillaries retreats, the **blue** light from your veins mixes visually with the **yellow** undertones of your skin"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                14
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2467,
                "end_index": 2519,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "As any art student knows, blue and yellow make green"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                11,
                15
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2522,
                "end_index": 2723,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **How Our Brains Process Health:** Evolutionary biology has wired the human brain to be incredibly sensitive to facial color as a way to detect if someone is a threat or carrying a contagious disease"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                1
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2725,
                "end_index": 2947,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Because red and green are complementary colors, when your brain notices a sudden, drastic drop in \"redness\" in someone's face, it visually overcompensates and perceives the remaining cool, pale undertones as \"sickly green\""
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                16,
                17
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 2955,
                "end_index": 3139,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "### Part 3: Real Biochemical Causes of a Green/Yellow Tint\nWhile \"nausea green\" is mostly optical, there are specific medical conditions where the body chemically turns yellowish-green"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                18
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3142,
                "end_index": 3329,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **Jaundice (Bilirubin Buildup):** If you have a liver, gallbladder, or blood disorder, your body cannot properly process **bilirubin** (a byproduct of old red blood cells breaking down)"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                17,
                18
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3331,
                "end_index": 3491,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Bilirubin is a yellow-green pigment; when it builds up in your tissues, it causes your skin and the whites of your eyes to turn a distinct, literal yellow-green"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                16,
                19
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3493,
                "end_index": 3621,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "* **\"Green Sickness\" (Severe Anemia):** Historically, a condition called *chlorosis* (or \"green sickness\") was widely documented"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                16,
                19
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3623,
                "end_index": 3676,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Today, we know this was severe iron-deficiency anemia"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                20,
                16
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3678,
                "end_index": 3855,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Because the patients lacked hemoglobin (the iron-rich compound that makes blood red), their skin lost all pinkness, causing them to develop a striking greenish-yellow complexion"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                21
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 3907,
                "end_index": 4001,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "The trope of a character turning neon green right before they vomit is a pop-culture shorthand"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                21
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 4003,
                "end_index": 4154,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Early animators exaggerated the subtle, real-life greenish-gray tint of nausea to make it immediately obvious to the audience that a character was sick"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            },
            {
              "confidence_scores": null,
              "grounding_chunk_indices": [
                1,
                21
              ],
              "segment": {
                "start_index": 4156,
                "end_index": 4363,
                "part_index": null,
                "text": "Additionally, green has long been culturally associated with decay, bile, and poisons (like historical green arsenic wallpaper), cementing \"bright green\" as the universal symbol for feeling under the weather"
              },
              "rendered_parts": null
            }
          ],
          "retrieval_metadata": null,
